#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119924 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119498 :question/id #uuid "7b28b42f-0ac4-543e-baeb-51534aaad480" :question/math-academy-id "e-10907" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}_1 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}4 \\\\ - 1 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}, \\qquad\n\\mathbf{a}_2 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]},\\qquad\n\\mathbf{a}_3 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ - 1 \\\\ 7\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\mathbf{a}_1 - \\mathbf{a}_2 -2 \\mathbf{a}_3 = \\mathbf{0}.$\n\nII. $x_1\\mathbf{a}_1 + x_2\\mathbf{a}_2 + x_3\\mathbf{a}_3  = \\mathbf{0}$ only if $x_1=x_2=x_3 = \\mathbf{0}.$\n\nIII. The set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2, \\mathbf{a}_3\\}$ is linearly independent." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2$ and $\\mathbf{v}_3$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 + x_3\\mathbf{v}_3= \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis $x_1 = x_2 = x_3 = \\mathbf{0}.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}-{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}-2{\\mathbf{a}}_{3} & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -14\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4+0-4 \\\\ -1-1+2 \\\\ 0+0-14\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -14\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\ne \\mathbf{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The equation $x_1\\mathbf{a}_1 + x_2\\mathbf{a}_2 + x_3\\mathbf{a}_3 = \\mathbf{0}$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}+2{x}_{3} &=\\mathbf{0} \\\\ -{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=\\mathbf{0} \\\\ 7{x}_{3} &=\\mathbf{0}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNotice that we can reorder the equations in this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=\\mathbf{0} \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+2{x}_{3} &=\\mathbf{0} \\\\ 7{x}_{3} &=\\mathbf{0}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system using back substitution, we find that the solution is $x_1 = x_2 = x_3 = \\mathbf{0}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2, \\mathbf{a}_3 \\}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb5ca50-e9f3-5513-aff1-1ada8c225a42" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050267 :topic/difficulty 0.6911657375018757 :topic/encompasses [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428062 :encompassing/rationale "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account differential: 1 rises / 1 eligible passed source activities = 1.000000; nearest selected fraction = 1. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: differential=1 (1/1). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: False. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/differential-frequency-estimates.json, pair 1047->148. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." :encompassing/topic {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050765} :encompassing/weight 1.0}] :topic/id #uuid "3afe28a0-5051-52a1-a085-a06727c301b8" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096669} {:db/id #edb/ref 175921860