end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe homogeneous vector equation gives the coordinate system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x_{1}-2x_{2} &=0 \\\\ -2x_{2}-x_{3} &=0 \\\\ 0 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nChoose one coefficient nonzero and solve the remaining coordinate equations by substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x_{2}&=1 \\\\ x_{1}-2(1)&=0\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x_{1}=2 \\\\ -2(1)-x_{3}&=0\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x_{3}=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives a nontrivial solution. Verify it directly:\n\n$$\n2\\mathbf{a}_{1}+\\mathbf{a}_{2}-2\\mathbf{a}_{3}=2\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nThe coefficients are not all zero, so the set is **linearly dependent**.\n\nA failed candidate alone does not prove independence; the homogeneous-equation argument settles the classification.\n\nThe statement “$\\mathbf{a}_{1}+\\mathbf{a}_{2}+2\\mathbf{a}_{3}=\\mathbf{0}$.” is **false**. The statement “$x_{1}\\mathbf{a}_{1}+x_{2}\\mathbf{a}_{2}+x_{3}\\mathbf{a}_{3}=\\mathbf{0}$ only if $x_{1}=x_{2}=x_{3}=0$.” is **false**. The statement “The set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_{1}, \\mathbf{a}_{2}, \\mathbf{a}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent.” is **false**."}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent Sets by Inspection"}]}
